Pelvic organ prolapse
Obstetrics Gynaecology & Womens health
Vaginal bulge or pressure, Visible tissue protrusion at introitus, Need to splint to defecate

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OGW-031
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of a visible vaginal mass causing severe chafing, bleeding, and complete inability to defecate.
Location: Nursing home
Present at Visit: Patient and nursing staff
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute irreducibility of a known pelvic organ prolapse, accompanied by superficial mucosal bleeding from chafing, and severe lower abdominal pain secondary to severe, unresolvable constipation (5 days). Urgent after-hours assessment was required to manually reduce the prolapse to relieve ischemia/bleeding, and to assist with acute bowel disimpaction that could not wait for the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: The lump has been out all day, it is bleeding onto my pad, and my stomach is cramping terribly because I cannot open my bowels.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a long-standing sensation of a physical bulge, heaviness, and dragging pressure in the vagina. This has acutely worsened today following a bout of severe coughing, resulting in a visible and palpable tissue mass protruding past the vaginal introitus that the patient cannot manually reduce. The patient reports a chronic need to apply manual digital pressure to the perineum or posterior vaginal wall to facilitate defecation (splinting). Today, despite splinting, she is completely unable to pass stool and complains of severe, cramping lower abdominal pain and lower back pain radiating to the groin. She notes improvement of symptoms historically when in a supine position, but today the mass remains exteriorized.
Functional Status: Immobile in bed due to discomfort and fear of worsening the bleeding.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of mucosal ulceration and fecal impaction.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 mmHg | HR: 92 bpm | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Patient is distressed, shifting uncomfortably, and acutely anxious regarding the bleeding mass.
Physical Exam: Abdominal exam reveals a distended, firm, and tender left lower quadrant consistent with severe fecal loading. Genitourinary inspection reveals a large, stage III posterior vaginal wall prolapse (rectocele) protruding through the introitus. The dependent mucosa is erythematous with superficial excoriations and mild contact bleeding due to chafing against undergarments. Digital rectal examination confirms a large, hard fecal impaction locked against the prolapsed posterior vaginal wall.
Environmental Observations: Pad heavily soiled with serosanguinous fluid.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking daily senna; insufficient for current impaction.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Rectocele (Pelvic Organ Prolapse) complicated by fecal impaction and mucosal excoriation - Status: Acute mechanical complication
Differentials:

Vaginal Vault Malignancy - Ruled out by direct visual inspection confirming the mass is everted, rugated vaginal mucosa, not a friable neoplastic growth.

Incarcerated Uterine Prolapse - While prolapse is present, the primary presenting complication is posterior (rectocele) causing bowel obstruction, rather than isolated apical/uterine descent causing urinary or ischemic issues.

Acute Diverticulitis - Unlikely as the primary cause of pain, given the direct tactile evidence of massive fecal impaction and the absence of fever or localized peritonism.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute mechanical crisis driven by a severe rectocele. The objective clinical findings of a large, protruding vaginal mass with superficial bleeding, combined with a confirmed hard fecal impaction, explain the acute abdominal cramping and distress. Urgent after-hours intervention was clinically mandated to perform manual disimpaction and subsequent reduction of the prolapse. The mechanical obstruction to defecation, exacerbated by the prolapse trapping the stool, required immediate physical relief to prevent stercoral ulceration or bowel perforation, satisfying MBS criteria for critical, non-delayable intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered a hypertonic phosphate enema. Following partial softening, performed gentle manual disimpaction yielding a large volume of hard stool, immediately resolving the severe left lower quadrant pain. Applied a thick layer of zinc oxide barrier cream to the excoriated vaginal mucosa and gently manually reduced the rectocele using water-based lubricant. Prescribed a robust bowel regimen: Macrogol 3350 (Movicol) two sachets daily.
Positive Behavioral Change: To prevent the bowel from locking the prolapse outside your body, drink exactly one large glass of water every time you take your new laxative powder; ensuring adequate hydration will allow the medication to keep the stool soft enough to pass without severe splinting. To protect the raw skin, remain strictly lying flat in bed for the rest of the evening; feeling the immediate reduction in pelvic dragging will reinforce the necessity of gravity relief.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Nursing staff instructed on daily barrier cream application.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not applicable.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 days to assess bowel function and arrange a formal urogynecology referral for definitive prolapse management.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Nursing staff to transfer the patient to the Emergency Department immediately if the prolapse falls out and turns dark purple or black, if the patient develops a fever, or if she begins vomiting continuously.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The manual reduction and disimpaction are temporary measures; long-term surgical or pessary management is required.
Clinical Course: The abdominal cramping should be entirely resolved. The raw skin on the prolapse will heal within a few days if kept clean, lubricated, and internal.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Inform the nursing staff immediately if you experience severe, new abdominal pain, or if the lump falls out and becomes extremely painful.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The wall between your bowel and your vagina has weakened, allowing your bowel to bulge forward (a rectocele). Hard stool became trapped in this bulge, pushing the whole mass outside your body and causing the bleeding from rubbing. By clearing the blockage, we removed the weight, allowing the prolapse to be pushed back inside. You must take the strong laxatives daily to prevent this from happening again.
Actionable Steps: Take the new laxative powder exactly as prescribed by the nurses. Drink plenty of fluids. Stay flat in bed tonight to allow the inflamed tissues to rest.

SMS INFORMATION

Presumptive diagnosis: Prolapse with fecal impaction. Bowel cleared. Prolapse reduced. Start strong daily laxatives. GP review in 3 days.

SMS EDUCATION

Take Movicol daily with plenty of water. Rest flat in bed tonight. Notify staff if prolapse falls out and turns purple or black.
